// @error expected i32, got string
// THE IMPLICIT RETURN WAS NEVER CHECKED AGAINST THE DECLARED TYPE.
//
// `checkRetStmtNode` has always checked an EXPLICIT `return <expr>` against `T.curRet` —
// `function h(): i32 { return "s" }` is a clean reject. The bare trailing expression, which
// IS the implicit return, was not checked at all. So this file's body type-checked clean and
// emitted a module that does not instantiate:
//
//   i32    declared, string body  ->  vl check rc 0, "expected i32, found (ref $type)"
//   string declared, i32 body     ->  vl check rc 0, invalid wasm
//   f64    declared, string body  ->  vl check rc 0, invalid wasm
//   A      declared, i32 body     ->  vl check rc 0, invalid wasm
//   boolean declared, i32 body    ->  RAN, printing `true` — a silently WRONG VALUE, which
//                                     is worse than a module that refuses to load
//
// All five are pre-existing on master, and none is a codegen bug: the checker accepted a
// program it should have refused.
//
// TWO THINGS HAD TO MOVE TOGETHER. The tail is now SEEDED with the declared type before it
// is checked, exactly as `checkRetStmtNode` seeds an explicit return — without that a
// narrower literal types at its DEFAULT and the check would reject `function five(): i64 5`,
// which `bare-expr-return-coercion.vl` pins as legal. And the check itself goes through
// `assignableExpr`, NOT `assignable`: the coercions a value gets when it meets a declared
// type live there (`boolean-to-i32.vl` says so outright), and the bare-expression return is
// one more such position. Using plain `assignable` reddens 13 corpus files that are pinning
// legitimate coercions — literal widening, a boolean meeting an i32, litunion adoption.
//
// AND THE TAIL MUST BE REACHABLE. `function early(): i32 { return 1 print(9) }` is two
// statements on one line: the `return` supplies the value and `print(9)` is dead code.
// `return-then-statement-same-line.vl` pins that and records that it "previously surfaced a
// spurious `expected i32, got null`" — which is exactly what a tail-position check without a
// divergence guard re-introduces. The guard is `daBranchDiverges` on the preceding statement.
function h(): i32 {
  "s"
}

print(h())
